    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Chimeric Antigen Receptor (CAR) T cell therapies have proven efficacious for patients with chemotherapy-refractory cancers. However, due to the transactivation of various immune cells and the development of a hyper-inflammatory disease state, many patients experience life-threatening complications post-CAR-T cell therapy, such as immune effector cell associated neurotoxicity syndrome (ICANS), immune effector cell associated hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis (IEC-HS), and cytokine release syndrome (CRS). There is a strong clinical need to identify robust biomarkers that enable the early diagnosis and treatment of this emerging class of immune-effector induced conditions. In this thesis, I propose a novel platform for the identification of robust and specific immune-cell-related biomarkers using exosomes, a subclass of extracellular vesicles, isolated from patient plasma samples. Specifically, the present work aims to develop and characterize a novel liquid biopsy capable of isolating T-cell, natural killer cell, and macrophage derived exosomes from a 1 mL sample of plasma, which is feasibly implementable in clinical workflows. By characterizing the isolated morphology, size range, and protein contents of isolated vesicles, I demonstrate that my proposed method isolates exosomes with greater purity than current isolation methods, including ultracentrifugation and size exclusion chromatography, and serves as a feasible method of identifying cytokine cargo in exosomes. With further optimization, we expect this can be developed into a potential clinical test of immune-effector adverse conditions.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Scala, J., Snyder, M., and Frank, M. (2025). Creating a Novel Liquid Biopsy Using Exosomes: A Platform for Immunological Biomarker Discovery. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/mx526yw0359. https://doi.org/10.25740/mx526yw0359.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
